# /etc/mkinitcpio.conf - Example configuration for kira integration
#
# This is an example mkinitcpio configuration file showing how to integrate
# kira TPM2-based TOTP authentication into the early boot process.
#
# Copy the relevant HOOKS line to your /etc/mkinitcpio.conf and adjust as needed.

# KIRA SIGNING KEY ON A YUBIKEY
# With 'kira setup' on a YubiKey, the approval that runs after every image
# build signs on the token. The PIN for it is not here: 'kira control'
# stores it in /etc/kira/pin, readable by root alone, and nothing puts it
# into the image.

# MODULES
# The following modules are loaded before any boot hooks are run.
MODULES=()

# BINARIES
# This setting includes any additional binaries a given user may wish into
# the CPIO image.
BINARIES=()

# FILES
# This setting is similar to BINARIES above, however, files are added as-is
# and are not parsed in any way.
FILES=()

# HOOKS
# This is the most important setting in this file. The HOOKS control the
# modules and scripts added to the image, and what happens at boot time.
# Order is important, and it is recommended that you do not change the order
# in which HOOKS are added.

# SYSTEMD INITRAMFS WITH KIRA AND ENCRYPTION
# sd-kira requires a systemd-based initramfs with sd-encrypt; the order
# of the two does not matter. The passphrase is asked at kira's prompt
# for every volume whose key file is /run/kira/unlock.sock, set on the
# kernel command line as rd.luks.key=<UUID>=/run/kira/unlock.sock (see
# the README, "Configure the disk unlock").
HOOKS=(base systemd autodetect modconf block keyboard sd-kira sd-encrypt filesystems fsck)

# OTHER COMMON CONFIGURATIONS
# Here are some other common HOOKS configurations that include kira:

# With LVM and encryption
#HOOKS=(base systemd autodetect modconf block keyboard sd-kira sd-encrypt sd-lvm2 filesystems fsck)

# With LUKS on LVM
#HOOKS=(base systemd autodetect modconf block sd-lvm2 keyboard sd-kira sd-encrypt filesystems fsck)

# Multiple encrypted devices
#HOOKS=(base systemd autodetect modconf block keyboard sd-kira sd-encrypt filesystems fsck)

# COMPRESSION
# Use this to compress the initramfs image. By default, gzip compression
# is used. Use 'cat' to create an uncompressed image.
#COMPRESSION="gzip"
#COMPRESSION="bzip2"
#COMPRESSION="lzma"
#COMPRESSION="xz"
#COMPRESSION="lzop"
#COMPRESSION="lz4"
#COMPRESSION="zstd"

# COMPRESSION_OPTIONS
# Additional options for the compressor
#COMPRESSION_OPTIONS=()

# IMPORTANT NOTES FOR KIRA INTEGRATION:
#
# 1. Hook Placement:
#    - Place sd-kira next to sd-encrypt, in either order: the units
#      decide that the TOTP code is shown before the disk is unlocked
#    - Place after 'keyboard' to ensure input is available if needed
#
# 2. Use Case:
#    - kira hooks are primarily useful with encrypted filesystems
#    - They provide TOTP codes during the boot process for authentication
#    - Most useful when you need 2FA before unlocking encrypted drives
#
# 3. Prerequisites:
#    - Install kira binary: make install
#    - Install hooks: make install-mkinitcpio
#    - Seal TOTP secret: kira slot add
#      (You will be prompted to enter an optional password securely)
#
# 4. Hook Behavior:
#    - The hook installs a systemd unit that runs 'kira-boot run' during boot
#    - This provides continuous TOTP code display
#    - No kernel parameters or manual intervention needed
#
# 5. Compatibility:
#    - sd-kira requires a systemd-based initramfs ('systemd' in HOOKS)
#      with sd-encrypt; kira provides the key of the volumes whose
#      key file is /run/kira/unlock.sock (rd.luks.key= on the kernel
#      command line)
#
# 6. After Configuration:
#    - Rebuild initramfs: sudo mkinitcpio -P
#    - Reboot to test the integration
#
# For detailed documentation, see the project README.md
